<figure id="S5.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.08457v1/iclr2026/images/resp_len_benchmark_v2.png" id="S5.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/274;" width="548" height="274" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span><span id="S5.F8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Response length comparison across multimodal and textual benchmarks.</span>
We report the average number of generated tokens for three model variants
(ARES-CS-Vanilla, ARES-CS-7B, and ARES-RL-7B). RL training consistently
reduces response length on most benchmarks, indicating improved reasoning
efficiency. In contrast, response length increases on the most challenging
datasets—AIME25 (textual) and OlympiadBench (multimodal)—highlighting the
adaptive behavior of our RL approach: trimming unnecessary reasoning on easy
problems while encouraging deeper exploration on difficult ones.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span> <span id="S5.T5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation study of Dynamic KL Loss and Entropy Reward.</span>
Building upon our Cold Start stage. Best results per column are <span id="S5.T5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>
and second-best are <span id="S5.T5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span>.</figcaption>
