<li id="bib.bib100" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">Zou et al. (2025)</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">
Chengke Zou, Xingang Guo, Rui Yang, Junyu Zhang, Bin Hu, and Huan Zhang.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">Dynamath: A dynamic visual benchmark for evaluating mathematical reasoning robustness of vision language models, 2025.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">URL <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.00836" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.00836</a>.

</span></li>
    
</ul>
</section>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix</h2>

<section id="Ax1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Appendix Contents</h3>

</section>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Implementation Details</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our models, based on Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-VL-3B-Instruct, are trained using a two-stage strategy. The first stage is a cold-start supervised fine-tuning (SFT), where we independently fine-tune the large language model (LLM) module for two epochs. This stage uses a batch size of 256, a sequence length of 32k, and a learning rate of <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>. Following SFT, we further optimize the model using Adaptive Entropy Policy Optimization (AEPO). In each AEPO iteration, we sample 512 prompts from the training set and generate eight rollouts per prompt, yielding 4,096 trajectories. Rollouts are generated with a temperature of 1.0 and a top-p of 0.99, and the maximum sequence length is set to 20k tokens (a 4k token prompt and a 16k token response). For policy updates, we use a global batch size of 128 and the AdamW optimizer with a learning rate of <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> and a weight decay of <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>, without a learning rate warmup. To ensure training stability, we apply online filtering to discard samples whose mean overall reward falls outside the predefined range of [0.01, 0.99]. This process removes outliers that could distort advantage estimation and impede policy updates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Token-Level Entropy Measurement</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify the model’s local uncertainty during generation, we compute the <em id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">token-level entropy</em> at step <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="A2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H_{t}=-\sum_{j=1}^{V}p_{t,j}\log p_{t,j},\qquad p_{t}=\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid q,o_{&lt;t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></munderover><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t}=-\sum_{j=1}^{V}p_{t,j}\log p_{t,j},\qquad p_{t}=\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid q,o_{&lt;t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the policy under parameters <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is the input query, and <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the prefix of generated tokens up to position <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The probability vector <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>V</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from the normalized logits <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by a temperature-scaled softmax transformation:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}=\mathrm{Softmax}\!\left(\tfrac{z_{t}}{T}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:3.922em;" width="3.922em"><mi>Softmax</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}=\mathrm{Softmax}\!\left(\tfrac{z_{t}}{T}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with vocabulary size <math id="A2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and decoding temperature <math id="A2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> controlling the sharpness of the distribution.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Role in our setting.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike sequence-level measures (e.g., log-likelihood of the whole output), <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the fine-grained uncertainty at each generation step. During reinforcement learning, we treat <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as an intrinsic signal of “reasoning hesitation”: a higher entropy indicates that the model considers multiple plausible continuations, whereas a lower entropy suggests that the model is confident about the next token.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is tied to the distribution <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, not to the realized token <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> drawn from <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence two identical tokens generated at different timesteps may correspond to different entropies, depending on the local distributional context. In this sense, token entropy should be interpreted as a property of the model’s decision process rather than of the discrete outcome itself.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>RLVR Algorithms</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we provide additional details of the baseline RLVR algorithms used in our experiments, namely <em id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO)</em> and <em id="A3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Dynamic sAmpling Policy Optimization (DAPO)</em>. Both methods extend traditional policy optimization frameworks to improve the stability and effectiveness of reinforcement learning for reasoning tasks, yet they differ in how they handle variance reduction, KL regularization, and sampling dynamics.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO).</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>)</cite> extends conventional policy optimization by introducing group-wise normalization. Specifically, training samples are divided into <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> groups <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{G}_{1},\ldots,\mathcal{G}_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{G}_{1},\ldots,\mathcal{G}_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math> based on input prompts or task-specific criteria. For each group <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, both a policy <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and a frozen reference policy <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{ref}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{ref}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are maintained. The GRPO objective can be written as:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{G}_{i},\,y\sim\pi_{\theta}(y|x)}\Bigg[\min\Bigg(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y|x)}{\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{ref}}}(y|x)}\,\hat{A}(x,y),\;\mathrm{clip}\!\Big(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y|x)}{\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{ref}}}(y|x)},\,1-\epsilon,\,1+\epsilon\Big)\,\hat{A}(x,y)\Bigg)\Bigg]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.428em;" width="1.428em"><mi>clip</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{G}_{i},\,y\sim\pi_{\theta}(y|x)}\Bigg[\min\Bigg(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y|x)}{\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{ref}}}(y|x)}\,\hat{A}(x,y),\;\mathrm{clip}\!\Big(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y|x)}{\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{ref}}}(y|x)},\,1-\epsilon,\,1+\epsilon\Big)\,\hat{A}(x,y)\Bigg)\Bigg],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> controls the size of the trust region. The group-relative advantage <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}(x,y_{i})\;=\;\frac{r(x,y_{i})-\mathrm{mean}(\{r(x,y_{1}),\ldots,r(x,y_{G})\})}{\mathrm{std}(\{r(x,y_{1}),\ldots,r(x,y_{G})\})+\epsilon}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>std</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}(x,y_{i})\;=\;\frac{r(x,y_{i})-\mathrm{mean}(\{r(x,y_{1}),\ldots,r(x,y_{G})\})}{\mathrm{std}(\{r(x,y_{1}),\ldots,r(x,y_{G})\})+\epsilon}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This relative advantage stabilizes training by normalizing each sample’s reward against the variance of its group. By leveraging intra-group normalization, GRPO encourages responses that are better than their peers, while maintaining diversity across groups.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Dynamic sAmpling Policy Optimization (DAPO).</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, DAPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> removes the explicit KL penalty and instead incorporates several mechanisms for improved sample efficiency. First, it introduces a <em id="A3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">clip-higher</em> strategy to reduce over-penalization of promising trajectories. Second, it adopts <em id="A3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dynamic sampling</em>, where response sampling probabilities are adaptively adjusted based on entropy signals. Third, DAPO employs token-level policy gradient updates with overlong reward shaping. The DAPO objective is formulated as:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{\mathrm{DAPO}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{(q,a)\sim\mathcal{D},\,\{o^{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}(\cdot|q)}\left[\frac{1}{\sum_{i=1}^{G}|o^{i}|}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\sum_{t=1}^{|o^{i}|}\min\!\Big(r^{i}_{t}(\theta),\,\mathrm{clip}(r^{i}_{t}(\theta),1-\epsilon_{\mathrm{low}},1+\epsilon_{\mathrm{high}})\,\hat{A}^{i}_{t}\Big)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>DAPO</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>o</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mpadded style="width:1.653em;" width="1.653em"><mi>min</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>clip</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>low</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>high</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{\mathrm{DAPO}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{(q,a)\sim\mathcal{D},\,\{o^{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}(\cdot|q)}\left[\frac{1}{\sum_{i=1}^{G}|o^{i}|}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\sum_{t=1}^{|o^{i}|}\min\!\Big(r^{i}_{t}(\theta),\,\mathrm{clip}(r^{i}_{t}(\theta),1-\epsilon_{\mathrm{low}},1+\epsilon_{\mathrm{high}})\,\hat{A}^{i}_{t}\Big)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{i}_{t}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{i}_{t}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as in GRPO, and <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}^{i}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}^{i}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the token-level relative advantage. Unlike GRPO, which focuses on group-level normalization, DAPO shifts optimization to the token level, enabling finer-grained control and adaptive shaping of the learning signal. This makes DAPO a strong baseline for RLVR without requiring a value network.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Both GRPO and DAPO contribute to reducing variance and stabilizing policy optimization in RLVR. GRPO leverages group-relative normalization, while DAPO refines the approach with token-level updates and dynamic sampling. In our work, we adopt DAPO as the primary baseline due to its strong empirical performance and its widespread use in prior RLVR literature.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Window-Entropy Aggregation</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While token-level entropy <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a fine-grained measure of uncertainty, single-token fluctuations are often dominated by lexical ambiguity or local randomness. To capture more coherent reasoning segments, we aggregate entropy over a sliding window of length <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bar{H}_{t:w}\;=\;\frac{1}{w}\sum_{\tau=t}^{t+w-1}H_{\tau}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}_{t:w}\;=\;\frac{1}{w}\sum_{\tau=t}^{t+w-1}H_{\tau}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This windowed statistic smooths out spurious token-level spikes and highlights regions where the model persistently maintains high uncertainty.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A window is flagged as <em id="A4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">high-entropy</em> if its average entropy exceeds a dynamic threshold <math id="A4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, which is updated online to match the distributional scale of the current batch, which is shown in Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Window Entropy Serves as a Trigger for Exploration in MLRMs ‣ 2 Relations Between Token Entropy and Reasoning Difficulty ‣ ARES: Multimodal Adaptive Reasoning via Difficulty-Aware Token-Level Entropy Shaping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>. Intuitively, these windows correspond to stretches of the output where the model is genuinely uncertain about its reasoning trajectory, as opposed to momentary ambiguity on function words or symbols.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The aggregated entropy <math id="A4.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bar{H}_{t:w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}_{t:w}</annotation></semantics></math> therefore serves two purposes: (i) it localizes “hard thinking” phases within the output, providing a natural signal for allocating additional exploration, and (ii) it stabilizes training by reducing sensitivity to token-level noise. In our AEPO algorithm, only tokens falling inside validated high-entropy windows are granted relaxed KL budgets, thereby ensuring that extra computation is spent precisely where reasoning effort is most needed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>KL Penalty Inflates GRPO Advantage Variance compared to KL Loss</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a fixed prompt <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, GRPO samples a group of <math id="A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> responses <math id="A5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. from <math id="A5.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let the <em id="A5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">task<math id="A5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>entropy shaped return</em> be</p>
<table id="A5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}\;\triangleq\;r_{\mathrm{acc}}(x,y_{i})\;-\;\lambda_{d}\,\frac{C_{\mathrm{ent}}(x,y_{i})}{Z_{\mathrm{ent}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≜</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>acc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ent</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>ent</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}\;\triangleq\;r_{\mathrm{acc}}(x,y_{i})\;-\;\lambda_{d}\,\frac{C_{\mathrm{ent}}(x,y_{i})}{Z_{\mathrm{ent}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the sample-averaged per-token reference KL estimator be</p>
<table id="A5.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{i}\;\triangleq\;\frac{1}{L}\sum_{t=1}^{L}\mathrm{kld}_{t,i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≜</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><msub><mi>kld</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{i}\;\triangleq\;\frac{1}{L}\sum_{t=1}^{L}\mathrm{kld}_{t,i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We compare two GRPO implementations:</p>
<ol id="A5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:16pt;">
<li id="A5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">KL penalty</span> (merge KL into the return):</p>
<table id="A5.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}_{i}\;=\;S_{i}\;-\;\kappa K_{i},\qquad A^{\prime}_{i}\;=\;R^{\prime}_{i}\;-\;\bar{R}^{\prime},\quad\bar{R}^{\prime}=\tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}R^{\prime}_{j}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}_{i}\;=\;S_{i}\;-\;\kappa K_{i},\qquad A^{\prime}_{i}\;=\;R^{\prime}_{i}\;-\;\bar{R}^{\prime},\quad\bar{R}^{\prime}=\tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}R^{\prime}_{j}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">KL loss (actor-only)</span>:</p>
<table id="A5.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}\;=\;S_{i},\qquad A_{i}\;=\;R_{i}\;-\;\bar{R},\quad\bar{R}=\tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}R_{j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}\;=\;S_{i},\qquad A_{i}\;=\;R_{i}\;-\;\bar{R},\quad\bar{R}=\tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}R_{j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and add the separate actor regularizer <math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\kappa\cdot\frac{1}{L}\sum_{t}\beta_{t}\,\mathrm{kld}_{t,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>kld</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\cdot\frac{1}{L}\sum_{t}\beta_{t}\,\mathrm{kld}_{t,i}</annotation></semantics></math> which does <em id="A5.I1.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> enter <math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="A5.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="A5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(S_{i},K_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(S_{i},K_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> are i.i.d. with finite second moments, and denote</p>
<table id="A5.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{S}^{2}=\mathrm{Var}(S),\qquad\sigma_{K}^{2}=\mathrm{Var}(K),\qquad\mathrm{Cov}(S,K)=\rho\,\sigma_{S}\sigma_{K}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{S}^{2}=\mathrm{Var}(S),\qquad\sigma_{K}^{2}=\mathrm{Var}(K),\qquad\mathrm{Cov}(S,K)=\rho\,\sigma_{S}\sigma_{K}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Group-baseline advantage variance)<span id="Thmlemma1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For i.i.d. <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> with finite variance, the GRPO group-baseline advantage
<math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}=R_{i}-\bar{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}=R_{i}-\bar{R}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="A5.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(A_{i})\;=\;\Big(1-\tfrac{1}{N}\Big)\,\mathrm{Var}(R)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Var</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(A_{i})\;=\;\Big(1-\tfrac{1}{N}\Big)\,\mathrm{Var}(R).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Write <math id="A5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}=(1-\tfrac{1}{N})R_{i}-\tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{j\neq i}R_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}=(1-\tfrac{1}{N})R_{i}-\tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{j\neq i}R_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and use i.i.d. plus independence across indices to expand <math id="A5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(A_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(A_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>; cross-terms cancel and equation <a href="#A5.E7" title="In Lemma 1 (Group-baseline advantage variance). ‣ Appendix E KL Penalty Inflates GRPO Advantage Variance compared to KL Loss ‣ ARES: Multimodal Adaptive Reasoning via Difficulty-Aware Token-Level Entropy Shaping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(KL penalty inflates GRPO advantage variance)<span id="Thmproposition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under the assumptions above,</span></p>
<table id="A5.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(A^{\prime}_{i})-\mathrm{Var}(A_{i})\;=\;\Big(1-\tfrac{1}{N}\Big)\,\Big(\kappa^{2}\sigma_{K}^{2}\;-\;2\kappa\,\mathrm{Cov}(S,K)\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn> 2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Cov</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(A^{\prime}_{i})-\mathrm{Var}(A_{i})\;=\;\Big(1-\tfrac{1}{N}\Big)\,\Big(\kappa^{2}\sigma_{K}^{2}\;-\;2\kappa\,\mathrm{Cov}(S,K)\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, if <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}(S,K)\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}(S,K)\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math> (empirically common), then
<math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(A^{\prime}_{i})-\mathrm{Var}(A_{i})\approx\big(1-\tfrac{1}{N}\big)\kappa^{2}\sigma_{K}^{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(A^{\prime}_{i})-\mathrm{Var}(A_{i})\approx\big(1-\tfrac{1}{N}\big)\kappa^{2}\sigma_{K}^{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
More generally, unless <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\in\big(0,\,2\,\mathrm{Cov}(S,K)/\sigma_{K}^{2}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn> 2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Cov</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\in\big(0,\,2\,\mathrm{Cov}(S,K)/\sigma_{K}^{2}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> (a narrow interval when <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}(S,K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}(S,K)</annotation></semantics></math> is small), one has <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(A^{\prime}_{i})&gt;\mathrm{Var}(A_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(A^{\prime}_{i})&gt;\mathrm{Var}(A_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 (Group-baseline advantage variance). ‣ Appendix E KL Penalty Inflates GRPO Advantage Variance compared to KL Loss ‣ ARES: Multimodal Adaptive Reasoning via Difficulty-Aware Token-Level Entropy Shaping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, it suffices to compare <math id="A5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(R^{\prime}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(R^{\prime}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(R_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(R_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We have <math id="A5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(R_{i})=\mathrm{Var}(S)=\sigma_{S}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(R_{i})=\mathrm{Var}(S)=\sigma_{S}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(R^{\prime}_{i})\;=\;\mathrm{Var}(S_{i}-\kappa K_{i})\;=\;\sigma_{S}^{2}+\kappa^{2}\sigma_{K}^{2}-2\kappa\,\mathrm{Cov}(S,K)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Cov</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(R^{\prime}_{i})\;=\;\mathrm{Var}(S_{i}-\kappa K_{i})\;=\;\sigma_{S}^{2}+\kappa^{2}\sigma_{K}^{2}-2\kappa\,\mathrm{Cov}(S,K).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Subtracting and multiplying by <math id="A5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\tfrac{1}{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\tfrac{1}{N})</annotation></semantics></math> yields equation <a href="#A5.E8" title="In Proposition 1 (KL penalty inflates GRPO advantage variance). ‣ Appendix E KL Penalty Inflates GRPO Advantage Variance compared to KL Loss ‣ ARES: Multimodal Adaptive Reasoning via Difficulty-Aware Token-Level Entropy Shaping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Consequences for gradient variance.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let the score-function term be <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}=\sum_{t=1}^{L}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{i,t}\mid s_{i,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}=\sum_{t=1}^{L}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{i,t}\mid s_{i,t})</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose, as is standard in variance analyses, that <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> are weakly correlated.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            This assumption is used for <em id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">comparative</em> purposes; the conclusion also holds under more general covariance decompositions.</span></span></span>
Then</p>
<table id="A5.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}\big(A^{\prime}_{i}G_{i}\big)\;\approx\;\mathrm{Var}(A^{\prime}_{i})\,\mathbb{E}\|G_{i}\|^{2}\;&gt;\;\mathrm{Var}(A_{i})\,\mathbb{E}\|G_{i}\|^{2}\;\approx\;\mathrm{Var}\big(A_{i}G_{i}\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}\big(A^{\prime}_{i}G_{i}\big)\;\approx\;\mathrm{Var}(A^{\prime}_{i})\,\mathbb{E}\|G_{i}\|^{2}\;&gt;\;\mathrm{Var}(A_{i})\,\mathbb{E}\|G_{i}\|^{2}\;\approx\;\mathrm{Var}\big(A_{i}G_{i}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">whenever <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(A^{\prime}_{i})&gt;\mathrm{Var}(A_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(A^{\prime}_{i})&gt;\mathrm{Var}(A_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> by Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (KL penalty inflates GRPO advantage variance). ‣ Appendix E KL Penalty Inflates GRPO Advantage Variance compared to KL Loss ‣ ARES: Multimodal Adaptive Reasoning via Difficulty-Aware Token-Level Entropy Shaping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
In the KL-loss implementation, the additional KL gradient
<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\sum_{t}\beta_{t}\nabla_{\theta}\mathrm{kld}_{t,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>kld</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\sum_{t}\beta_{t}\nabla_{\theta}\mathrm{kld}_{t,i}</annotation></semantics></math>
is <em id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> multiplied by the group advantage and therefore does not inherit its variance amplification; it also targets the trust-region deviation at a per-token granularity, improving credit assignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Merging per-token KL into the return injects a high-frequency, task-agnostic signal (<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) into the groupwise competition, thereby amplifying the variance of the advantage and the policy-gradient estimator. Using KL as an actor-only regularizer decouples the trust-region control from the task/entropy signals, keeping the advantage low-variance and allowing a dedicated controller to track the KL budget.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>The Algorithm workflow of AEPO</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide a detailed flowchart of our AEPO algorithm in the following diagram <a href="#algorithm1" title="In Appendix F The Algorithm workflow of AEPO ‣ ARES: Multimodal Adaptive Reasoning via Difficulty-Aware Token-Level Entropy Shaping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Adaptive-Entropy Policy Optimization (AEPO)</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">

<span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input :</span> 
Reference model <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>; initial policy <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>; prompts <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>; 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline">group size <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>; window size <math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>; rollout parameters (temperature, top-<math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>); 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline">entropy quantile <math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>; relaxation factor <math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>; 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline">difficulty buckets <math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\texttt{easy},\texttt{medium},\texttt{hard}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">easy</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">medium</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">hard</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\texttt{easy},\texttt{medium},\texttt{hard}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with KL targets <math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\delta_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\delta_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline">entropy weights <math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\lambda_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\lambda_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, base KL weights <math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\beta_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\beta_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and shaping functions <math id="algorithm1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>; 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline">GRPO clipping parameter <math id="algorithm1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>; controller step size <math id="algorithm1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output :</span> Updated policy <math id="algorithm1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
          <span id="algorithm1.4.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.8.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">each training iteration</em> <span id="algorithm1.4.8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
          Sample a mini-batch of prompts <math id="algorithm1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{b}\}_{b=1}^{B}\sim\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{b}\}_{b=1}^{B}\sim\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.10" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
          <span id="algorithm1.4.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">foreach</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.10.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prompt <math id="algorithm1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.11" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <span id="algorithm1.4.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(1) Rollout generation and entropy computation </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.12" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
          Generate <math id="algorithm1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> rollouts <math id="algorithm1.m20" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{o_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.13" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
          Compute token entropies <math id="algorithm1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> and sliding-window means <math id="algorithm1.m22" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bar{H}_{i,t:w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}_{i,t:w}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.14" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <span id="algorithm1.4.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(2) Dynamic thresholding </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.15" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
          <math id="algorithm1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}\leftarrow\mathrm{Quantile}_{q}(\{H_{i,t}\}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Quantile</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}\leftarrow\mathrm{Quantile}_{q}(\{H_{i,t}\}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="algorithm1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>; 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.16" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
          <math id="algorithm1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\leftarrow\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\leftarrow\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
<math id="algorithm1.m26" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="m_{i,t}\leftarrow\mathbb{I}\{\bar{H}_{i,t:w}\geq\theta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i,t}\leftarrow\mathbb{I}\{\bar{H}_{i,t:w}\geq\theta\}</annotation></semantics></math>;  <math id="algorithm1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{(i)}_{\mathrm{HE}}\leftarrow\sum_{t}m_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>HE</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{(i)}_{\mathrm{HE}}\leftarrow\sum_{t}m_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math>
